# Line World Mermaid

Read the [video contract](references/video-contract.md). Keep Mermaid's actual
notation and layout while adapting its presentation to the film.

1. Choose the smallest family that expresses the facts. Use wide sections for
   sequence, Gantt, timeline or Git history and taller sections for state or
   block chains. Preserve semantic shapes, arrow tips and relationship labels.
2. Author `.mmd` with useful invisible `accTitle` and `accDescr`. Default to no
   visible `title` statement or diagram heading. Keep intrinsic entity, axis,
   state, actor and unit labels. Use colorset1; justify any colorset2 mapping.
3. Use the copied palette styler for exact tokens, then render through the
   copied `scripts/animate_mermaid_svg.py` with `--animation none`. This plugin
   copy calls its dedicated video finish: borderless gray individual bodies,
   white outlined groups, red focus supplied by the compositor, and preserved
   quantitative geometry. Check nested labels on active red and inactive gray.
4. Set top-level and flowchart `htmlLabels: false`, and journey
   `textPlacement: old`. Measure the exported SVG at its actual target size.
   Adjust native font configuration, direction, actor spacing or the frame
   division when labels are small. Increase legend row spacing with font size.
5. Some pinned grammars need metadata repair after native rendering. For
   mindmap, Sankey, timeline, Kanban and block, keep the intended name and
   description in source comments if directives are rejected, render without
   the directive gate, add invisible SVG title/desc with referenced unique IDs,
   then verify the final metadata. Do not fabricate successful native support.
6. Hand off the real SVG, source, viewBox, semantic node IDs, ports and hash
   receipt. Namespace IDs and internal references on import. Let the parent
   clock reveal and move the diagram; disable independent SVG/CSS animation.
7. Inspect node labels, legends, markers, focus clearance, palette and repeated
   seeking in the composed frame. Reject a tiny diagram that merely fits.

Use [producer workflows](references/producer-workflows.md) only for the needed
family syntax and renderer/export operations. Preserve the video contract when
those general workflows describe standalone solid presentation or title cards.

Read [gentle motion](references/gentle-motion.md) for a static whole-group float.
Preserve native node/edge relationships and derive ports from displayed bounds.
